DO  -  10.1016/S0016-5085(19)37642-5
AB  -  Introduction: Neoadjuvant therapy is increasingly being used for localized pancreatic adenocarcinoma. While there is evidence supporting neoadjuvant systemic chemotherapy as well as chemoradiation, more evidence is needed to determine whether systemic chemotherapy with chemoradiation offers benefits over chemoradiation alone. This study compares the outcomes of neoadjuvant chemoradiation therapy with and without systemic chemotherapy in resectable and borderline resectable pancreatic cancers. Methods: This retrospective review evaluated patients with resectable and borderline resectable pancreatic adenocarcinoma who completed neoadjuvant chemoradiation therapy with and without systemic chemotherapy prior to surgical resection at our institution from 2004 to 2017. 149 patients met inclusion criteria, with 75 having resectable cancer and 74 having borderline resectable cancer. Outcome variables included recurrence free survival and overall survival rates at 1 and 3 years, as well as surgical resection margins and pattern of recurrence. Results: In resectable pancreatic carcinoma, 72% of patients achieved recurrence free survival at 1 year in the chemoradiation alone group compared to 78% in patients treated with systemic chemotherapy and chemoradiation (p=0.55). 28% of patients had recurrence free survival at 3 years in the chemoradiation alone group compared to 31% in patients who also received systemic chemotherapy (p=0.75). In both treatment groups, 92% of patients lived past 1 year (p=0.92), and 44% of patients survived at least 3 years (p=0.95). In borderline resectable pancreatic carcinoma, 50% of patients had recurrence free survival at 1 year in the chemoradiation alone group compared to 70% in patients who also received systemic chemotherapy (p=0.079). The 3 year recurrence free survival was 26% and 29% for the chemoradiation alone group and the systemic chemotherapy plus chemoradiation group respectively (p=0.85). There was no significant difference in 1 year overall survival: 85% of patients in the chemoradiation alone group survived compared to 92% in patients treated with systemic chemotherapy and chemoradiation (p=0.32). Both groups had 41% overall survival at 3 years (p=0.96). Discussion Among patients with resectable and borderline resectable pancreatic adenocarcinoma, there was no significant difference in overall or recurrence free survival between patients treated with chemoradiation with and without systemic chemotherapy. Our findings suggest that both systemic neoadjuvant chemotherapy with chemoradiation and chemoradiation alone are efficacious treatment options for localized pancreatic carcinoma. This brings into question whether more effective systemic chemotherapy is necessary to increase survival benefit. [Figure Presented] [Figure Presented]
