AB  -  Purpose/Objective(s): Patients (pts) with resectable pancreatic cancer are treated by primary surgery with adjuvant therapy, currently achieving about 20 months (m) median overall survival time (mOS). We tested whether neoadjuvant chemoradiation therapy (CRT) in resectable pancreatic cancer is superior compared to primary resection in this first multi‐center phase II RCT on this question. Materials/Methods: Eligibility required histologically proven ductal adenocarcinoma of the pancreatic head, <180≤ contact to peri‐pancreatic vessels. Randomization was: arm A ‐ primary surgery, arm B ‐ neoadjuvant CRT (55.4 Gy; gemcitabine/cisplatin), followed by surgery 6 weeks later. From 2005 a protocol amendment suggested adjuvant chemotherapy (gemcitabine). The primary endpoint was mOS. The proposed sample size of 254 eligible patients was planned to provide at least 80% power to detect a 4.33 m difference in mOS with a significance level of 5% in a 2‐ sided log‐rank test. Results: Seventy‐three pts were randomized in 8 centers (A n = 37, B n = 36) between June 2003 and December 2009 when due to slow recruitment the trial was closed. Data of 68 pts (A/B n = 34) could be evaluated, 3 pts withdrew consent (A/B n = 2/1), 1 center sent no data (A/ B n = 1). Allocated therapy was received by 33 pts in arm A and 30 pts in arm B. Sepsis and subsequent death before surgery occurred in 1 pt (arm A). Four pts refused the allocated CRT and underwent primary surgery. Another 4 pts (B) did not undergo surgery due to obvious tumor progression after CRT. Tumor resection was performed in 24 pts (A) and in 20 pts (B). Explorative laparotomy revealed locally non‐resectable tumor in 6 pts (A/B n = 4/2) and distant metastasis in 13 pts (A/B n = 5/8). The R0‐resection rate was 67% (16/24 pts) in A and 90% (18/20 pts) in B (p= 0.083). In arm A, 9/24 patients (38%) had pN0‐status versus 14/20 patients (70%) in arm B. One patient (A) died postoperatively due to perioperative myocardial infarction and multiorgan failure in hospital. Postoperative complications were comparable in both groups. Intention‐to‐treat mOS was 14.4 m (A) and 17.4 m (B) (p = n.s.). Analysis per protocol shows a median overall survival of 25 m in arm B versus 18 m in arm A for pts with resections (p = n.s.). Conclusions: Due to under‐recruitment mOS was not significantly different in the intention‐to‐treat analysis. However, there was a trend for a higher rate of clear resection margins after CRT and also for prolonged survival in arm B at analysis per protocol. Also, CRT helped to de‐mask the biology of the tumor sparing pts with rapidly progressive disease from surgery.
