AB  -  Categories Pancreas ‐ Malignant Abstract Introduction: Feasibility, benefits and challenges of neo‐adjuvant chemoradiotherapy (NACRT) remain unproven in the resource constraint setup of developing countries. Methods: In this single‐centre randomised trial, resectable or borderline resectable pancreatic cancer were randomly assigned to preoperative CRT or upfront surgery. NACRT consisted of 2 cycles of gemcitabine and Nab‐paclitaxel, followed by concurrent oral capecitabine + 25 fractions x 1.8 Gray Radiation, followed by surgery and 4 cycles of adjuvant therapy or upfront surgery and 6 cycles of adjuvant chemotherapy. The primary endpoint was overall survival. Results: From March 2019 to March 2021, 80 patients were randomly assigned into two groups; 41 in NACRT and 39 in upfront surgery. Recurrent cholangitis due to stent block was the foremost reason for dropout. The median overall survival was 18.90 months with neo‐adjuvant and 14.7 months in the upfront surgery group (HR, 0.78; 95% CI, 0.58 to 1.05; P = 0.096). The resection rate was 36/41 (87.80%) in the upfront surgery group versus 14/31 (45.61%) in the NACRT group (p= <0.001). The secondary endpoints favoured NACRT, R0 resection rate, 92.86% vs 75%; P = 0.18, pathologic lymph nodes, 00% vs 30.56%; P = 0.006, perineural invasion, 14.29% vs 36.11%; P = 0.14. Conclusion: The median overall survival and disease free survival showed an advantage with NACRT but did not reach statistical significance. The secondary endpoints favoured neo‐adjuvant treatment, but logistics, multiple visits, prolonged treatment, and financial constraints are some of the main hurdles for developing countries to deliver multimodality treatment.
