DO  -  10.1016/j.annonc.2021.05.468
AB  -  Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) has a poor 5‐year survival rate less than 10%. Recent randomized trials have confirmed that adjuvant chemotherapy using S‐1 in Japan (Uesaka et al. Lancet 2016;388:248‐57.) and modified FOLFIRINOX in the Western countries (Conroy et al. N Engl J Med 2018;379:2395‐406.) improves survival after pancreatectomy in patients with PDAC. Even after upfront surgical resection combined with adjuvant chemotherapy, which is the current standard of care, approximately 50% of patients develop disease recurrence within 2 years. Korean group reported that neoadjuvant chemoradiation improves overall survival better than upfront surgery in patients with borderline resectable PDAC (Jang et al. Ann Surg 2018;268:215‐222.). The latest multicenter randomized trial from Japan (PREP‐02/JSAP‐05) clearly suggested that neoadjuvant therapy using Gemcitabine+S‐1 in patients with resectable PDAC improves disease‐free survival and overall survival with less frequencies of lymph node metastasis and development of liver metastasis in resected patients (Unno et al. J Clin Oncol 2019;37:189, Satoi et al. J Clin Oncol 2019;37 (suppl; abstr 4126). Neoadjuvant therapy can be standard of care in patients with resectable and borderline resectable PDAC. JASPAC‐04 study revealed in a phase II setting that there was no difference in 2‐year progression‐free survival between S‐1 plus concurrent radiation and gemcitabine plus S‐1 in patients with resectable PDAC (J Clin Oncol 38, 2020 (suppl 4; abstr 724). Gemcitabine and S‐1 is the standard regimen of neoadjuvant therapy for patients with resectable PDAC in Japan. Several randomized controlled trials using FOLFIRINOX or gemcitabine plus nab‐paclitaxel as neoadjuvant therapy are going on, and their results will be appeared in a couple of years. Multimodal treatment including margin‐negative resection, but not surgical resection only, is the only way for cure in patients with PDAC.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-02303329/full
