AB  -  Background: Pancreatic cancer is the 10th leading cause of cancer death worldwide. For pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma the 5‐year survival ranges between 3‐16%. Surgical therapy offers the only cure for pancreatic adenocarcinoma. Local tumor progression even after R0‐resection still causes significant morbidity and mortality with local recurrence being as high as 50‐80%. This presented study hypothesizes that patients who receive IORT will have longer disease free survival and overall patient survival as well as lower recurrence rates with the same morbidity rates as compared to patients who did not receive IORT (primary hypothesis). The primary aim of this study is to examine the role of IORT in patients with resectable and borderline resectable pancreatic cancer (BRCP) using IORT prior to external beam radiotherapy (EBRT) in order to improve local control by dose escalation. Materials and Methods: Patients who are referred for pancreatic adenocarcinoma resection will be invited to participate in the presented trial. IORT will be applied to patients who undergo pancreatic resection. Patients will be divided into resectable and borderline resectable patients preoperatively and randomized prior to surgery into patients who receive IORT and those who do not. Randomization will take place in the OR after resectability status is confirmed. General resectability will be assessed prior to this using CT and MRT images done in routine evaluation prior pancreatic surgeries. (figure1) Results: Please see figure Conclusions: IORT might be a useful tool to ameliorate prognosis of patients undergoing pancreatic resection due to pancreatic adenocarcinoma. The presented study assessed this tool in order to gain knowledge in a scarcely evaluated field in hopes to contribute new findings for our patients.
