AB  -  Purpose/Objective(s): Surgery is the only curative modality for pancreatic cancer but is associated with significant morbidity. Neoadjuvant therapy is suggested to improve surgical outcomes in borderline resectable pancreatic cancer (BRPC) and may convert locally advanced pancreatic cancer (LAPC) patients to surgical candidates;however, factors associated with benefit from aggressive neoadjuvant therapy are unknown. Our goal was to identify tumor and treatment characteristics that may be useful in predicting pathologic response in BRPC and LAPC patients who received induction chemotherapy (CTX) and stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT). Materials/Methods: BRPC and LAPC patients (N =99) who underwent surgery from 2/2011‐1/2016 after induction chemo and 5‐fraction SBRT were retrospectively analyzed. Median dose of SBRT was 33 Gy (range, 25‐40 Gy). X2‐squared test was used to determine the difference in categorical distribution, and logistic regression evaluated associations between clinical factors and pathologic response. Complete pathologic response (pCR) was defined as no residual tumor at the time of surgery, and near pCR was defined as single cells of residual disease with marked fibrotic effect. The primary outcome was defined as near or complete pathologic response (npCR), compared to remaining patients. Other outcomes included margin‐negative (R0) resection. The model for multivariate logistic regression (MVA) included age >=65, head tumors, LAPC, >=4 months of CTX, >=30 Gy RT, and clinical trial participation. Results: LAPC comprised 48% of patients, 38% were male and median age was 62.5 years (range, 39.7‐83.6). The overall rates of pCR, npCR, and R0 resection were 8%, 27% and 88%, respectively. For patients who did and did not have npCR, there were more head lesions (86% vs 64%, P=.02), tumors >=3.0 cm (80% vs 69%, P ZNS), LAPC (66% vs 39%, P =0.01), administration of FOLFIRINOX‐based chemo (74% vs 69%, P =NS), clinical trial participation (34% vs 14%, P=0.02), and delivery of >=30 Gy SBRT (97% vs 88%, P =0.11) and a lower proportion of poorly differentiated tumors (26% vs 35%, P =NS) and receipt of >=4 months CTX (46% vs 55%, P =NS). On MVA, head lesion (OR 7.2, 95% CI: 2.0‐26.0, P =0.002) and LAPC (OR 5.7, 95% CI: 1.6‐20.6, P =0.008) were associated with npCR while the remaining factors were not significant. None of the above factors were associated with R0 status in this group. Conclusion: Among this select population of BRPC and LAPC patients who underwent resection after SBRT, head lesions, and LAPC were associated with improved pathologic response. This may reflect factors associated with response to therapy, as initially unresectable LAPC patients were converted to surgical candidates with majority (88%) achieving a R0 resection. Additional investigation is necessary to optimize pathologic response and long‐term outcomes.
