AB  -  Introduction: Pancreatic cancer (PC) is one of the most aggressive types of neoplasia. Is one of the fewdiseases that has no changed overall survival in recent years despite therapeutic efforts. It represents the fourth cause of death by cancer, with more than 100 deaths per year inwestern countries. Aim: To compare outcomes in patients with palliative double by pass due to locally advanced PAC treated or not with radiotherapy. Background: Surgery with curative intent has shown the best long‐term oncological results for the treatment of pancreatic adenocarcinoma (PAC). However,90%of these tumors are unresectable at the time ofdiagnosisdueto local or systemic disease. The first therapeutic attempts are minimally invasive or conventional procedures to palliate symptoms. The benefits of chemotherapy iswell described. However, there is controversy about the use of radiation therapy in patients with locally advanced PAC. Method: Method: Prospective cohort study of patients with palliative double by pass for locally advanced PAC between June 2006 and August 2015. The cohort was divided in 2 groups: treated with postoperative radiotherapy vs non‐exposed group. The standard chemotherapy protocol for locally advanced PAC was based on Gemcitabine 1000 mg/m2 + Oxaliplatin 85mg/m2 every 14 days. The radiotherapy protocol includes total doses of 45‐50.4 Gy (fraction was given by 1.8/2 Gy, 5 days a week). Results: 74 patients were included, 36 in the radiotherapy group and 38 in the non‐exposed group. Surgical complications in the non‐exposed group was 15.8% vs. 25% in the exposed group (p=0.325). Therewere no differences between postoperative complications and the indication of receiving radiotherapy (p=0.325). More than 98% of complications of radiotherapy were mild (nausea or diarrhea) without hospitalization, and 85% of patients completed the treatment. Overall survival at 1 and 2 years for patients in the non‐exposed group was 50% and 28%; and 64% and 35% respectively in the exposed group (p = 0.11) (power 72%). HR 0.53 (CI95%:0.24‐1.18) Discussion: The goal of palliative treatment in advanced pancreatic cancer are palliative treatmet of symptoms and oncological treatmet to improve survival. There are controversies in endoscopic managment of LAPC in early stages for patients with good performance status, because they probably need to repeat endoscopic procedures or even a surgery. The postoperative complications we had were mostly mild and were not a reason to delay or not to start oncological treatment in a timely manner. Most of the surgicall bypasses were made incidentaly in our series, due to the tendency to place endoscopic stents when unresectable in the preoperative is well known. When symptoms have been treated palliatively, it is necessary to complement an especific oncological treatment In order to generate a positive impact on survival. Postoperative radiotherapy after double by pass operation is used based on evidence often controversial. The indication is not uniform either, since it depends fundamentally of the custom of the treating team and there is a lack of consensus between physicians (oncologists and surgeons). Conclusion: W‐eve observed a significant clinical improve in the survival in patients with postoperative radiotherapy. However w‐eve not had enough power to demonstrate this difference, possible due to the small sample size. It is indispensable to develop randomized and prospective trials to guide more.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-01462359/full
