AB  -  Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) has a remarkable trend to metastasize early. Accordingly, there is a strong rational to investigate neoadjuvant chemotherapy in patients with resectable disease. We conducted a multicenter randomized phase II trial (PACT‐15; NCT01150630) to assess the role of combination chemotherapy in perioperative setting. Treatment‐naïve patients with 18e75 yr, KPS >60 pathologically confirmed stage I‐II resectable PDAC were randomized to 1. surgery followed by 6 cycles of adjuvant gemcitabine 1000 mg/m21,8,15 q4w (arm A), 2. surgery followed by 6 cycles of adjuvant PEXG (cisplatin 30 mg/m2, epirubicin 30 mg/m2, and gemcitabine 800 mg/m2 1,15 q4w and capecitabine 1250 mg/m2/day 1e28) (arm B), 3. neoadjuvant CT made by 3 cycles of PEXG before surgery followed by 3 cycles after surgery (arm C). The primary endpoint was 1 year event‐free survival (EFS); the secondary endpoints were EFS, overall survival (OS), and to assess the differences between arm A + B and arm C in pathological findings. With H0 40%; H1 55% (a 0.088; b 0.19) 54 patients per arm were needed. Due to the slow accrual rate the study was prematurely closed. Between Sep 2010 and April 2015, 88 eligible patients were randomized in 8 centers (arm A: 26, B: 31, C: 31). Basal patients and tumor characteristics were well balanced across arms. Pathological results are summarized in the table. Conclusions: Patients receiving neoadjuvant chemotherapy had longer EFS and OS as compared to those receiving adjuvant treatment. Albeit the trial was prematurely concluded, it provides the strongest piece of evidence currently available in favor of neoadjuvant chemotherapy in resectable PDAC.
