AB  -  Background: Concrete evidence on the best treatment strategy for borderline resectable pancreatic cancer (BRPC) is still absent. The aim of this study was to compare the treatment outcome of neoadjuvant treatment followed by surgical resection with upfront surgery in BRPCthrough a randomized prospective trial. Methods:A multicenter prospective randomized phase II/III trial was designed to enroll 110 BRPC patients from 4 tertiary referral hospitals in Korea. 2‐year survival rate (YSR) was the primary endpoint. Overall survival, 1‐YSR, and R0 resection rate were secondary endpoints. Intention‐to‐treat analysis was performed and an interim analysis was scheduled after 50% enrollment. Results: At interim analysis, 27 patients were allocated to neoadjuvantgroup and 28 to upfront surgery group. The overall survival at 2‐year was 39.3% with median survival of 17 months. The 2‐YSR was significantly better in neoadjuvant group compared to that of upfront surgery group (46.6% vs. 31.0%, p=0.011). 1‐YSR for neoadjuvant and upfront surgery groups were 80.0% and 43.9%, respectively. The R0 resection rate was also significantly higher in neoadjuvant group (76.4% vs. 30%, p=0.004). Conclusion:Based on this prospective randomized trial, neoadjuvant chemoradiation should be considered first rather than upfront surgery when establishing treatment strategy for BRPC patient.
