AB  -  Background: Neoadjuvant therapy (NT) is increasingly utilized for patients with pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) and yet major reasons for not undergoing subsequent pancreatectomy are poorly understood. Given the importance of completing multimodality therapy, we investigated factors associated with failure to undergo surgical resection following NT for PDAC. Methods: SWOG S1505 was a multicenter phase II randomized controlled trial of preoperative mFOLFIRINOX or gemcitabine/nab‐paclitaxel prior to planned pancreatectomy for patients with potentially resectable PDAC. The association between clinical, demographic, and hospital‐level characteristics and receipt of surgical resection was estimated via multiple logistic regression. Differences in overall survival (OS) from 18 weeks post‐randomization (scheduled time of surgery) according to resection status were assessed via Cox regression models. Results: Among 102 eligible patients, 73 (71.6%) underwent successful pancreatectomy whereas 29 (28.4%) did not, primarily because of progression/metastatic disease (n=12) or toxicity during NT (n=10). On multivariable logistic regression, weight loss during NT (OR 0.34, 95% CI 0.11‐0.93) and the hospital’s city size (small: OR 0.24, 95%CI 0.07‐0.80, large: OR 0.28, 95% CI 0.10‐0.79, compared to medium) were associated with lower probability of surgical resection, whereas age, sex, race, BMI, performance status (PS), insurance status, US region, treatment arm, tumor location, chemotherapy delays/modifications, and hospital characteristics were not. Surgical resection following NT was associated with improved OS (median 23.8 vs 10.8 months, Figure) even after adjusting for grades 3‐5 AEs, PS, and BMI (HR 0.55, 95% CI 0.32‐0.95). Conclusion: Failure to undergo resection following NT is relatively common among patients with potentially resectable PDAC and associated with worse survival. While few predictive factors were identified in this secondary analysis of the SWOG S1505 randomized controlled, further research must focus on risk factors for PS decline during NT that precludes surgical resection so that patient‐centered interventions can be delivered or alternate treatment sequencing recommended. [Formula presented]
