AB  -  Background: Occult micro‐metastases at the time of diagnosis are very common in pancreatic cancer. Neoadjuvant therapy allows the immediate delivery of systemic therapy and currently represents the standard of care in patients with borderine resectable disease. However, its use in upfront resectable patients remains disputed. Neoadjuvant multi‐agent chemotherapy has demonstrated to improve survival in borderline resectable patients and is increasingly used for patients with resectable disease. The present study sought to examine the effect of neoadjuvant multi‐agent chemotherapy compared to upfront surgery in patients with clinical stage I‐II pancreatic cancer. Methods: Patients with clinical stage I‐II pancreatic adenocarcinoma who underwent multi‐agent chemotherapy followed by surgical exploration and/or pancreatectomy, or who died prior to planned surgery, as well as patients who underwent upfront pancreatectomy were identified from the National Cancer Data Base (2006–2014). Multi‐agent chemotherapy alone or followed by surgery was classified as neoadjuvant therapy. Multivariable logistic regression modeling was performed to identify predictors for pancreatectomy in patients who received neoadjuvant therapy. Propensity score models predicting the likelihood of receiving neoadjuvant therapy were created. Patients were matched based on propensity score. Survival analyses were performed using the Kaplan‐Meier method and log‐rank test. Results: Neoadjuvant multi‐agent chemotherapy was initiated in 1,613 (18.8%) patients and upfront surgery was performed in 6,964 (81.2%) patients. Following neoadjuvant multi‐agent chemotherapy, 14 (0.9%) patients died prior to surgery, 263 (16.3%) were explored, and 1,336 (82.8%) underwent surgery. Among patients who underwent neoadjuvant therapy, white race (vs. other race: Adjusted Odds Ratio (AOR), 2.01; p 37.0 U/ml: AOR: 1.91; p 37.0 U/ml (70.8% vs. 63.6%; p < 0.001), academic center (68.2% vs. 56.1%; p < 0.001), and high‐volume hospital (50.1% vs. 37.5%; p < 0.001) compared to upfront surgery. Median overall survival from diagnosis was 25.8 months for patients who underwent neoadjuvant multi‐agent chemotherapy with or without resection and 20.9 months for all patients who underwent upfront surgery. After matching, baseline characteristics were equally distributed across treatment groups, with 1,162 patients in each arm. Adjusted, overall survival was significantly better for patients treated with neoadjuvant therapy compared with upfront resected patients (24.8 vs. 21.8 month; log‐rank p < 0.001). Conclusion: This study, while non‐randomized, suggests that neoadjuvant multi‐agent chemotherapy may be associated with survival benefit for clinical stage I and II pancreatic cancer patients in selected cases. These findings highlight the need to include resectable pancreatic cancer patients in randomized clinical trials investigating neoadjuvant multi‐agent chemotherapy. [Figure presented]
