AB  -  Introduction: Quest to improve survival in periampullary carcinoma has lead to multimodality treatment of the disease with neoadjuvant chemotherapy with or without radiotherapy. Materials and Methods: The present study is an ongoing prospective randomized study from July 2015 to June 2018. Patients with resectable or borderline resectable periampullary carcinoma were randomized into two groups: Group 1 (neoadjuvant chemo‐radiotherapy (NACRT) followed by surgery) and Group 2 (upfront surgery). Outcomes in terms of resectability, R0 resection rate, intraoperative difficulty, blood loss and post‐operative complications were studied. Issues related to delivery of NACRT were also analysed. We also compared percentage of patients completing all the 3 arms of treatment in the Group 1. (Chemotherapy, radiotherapy and surgery) Results: 47 patients met the inclusion criteria. Mean age was 57®14 years. 31/47(66%) were male. 24/47 (51%)patients had carcinoma head of pancreas, 20/47(42%) had distal cholangiocarcinoma and 3 (6.4%) had duodenal carcinoma on histopathology. Most common presenting feature was jaundice (35/ 47). Mean CA 19‐9 value was 602®1183 IU/mL. After randomization, Group 1 had 23 patients and Group 2 had 24 patients. In Group 1, 16/23 were started NACRT of which 14 completed NACRT (6 have been operated, one patient expired due to hypoglycemic attack, and one patient is waiting for reassessment, one patient lost to follow up while 5 progressed to metastatic disease). Two patients are receiving NACRT. 13/16 have been reassessed. On reassessment, 6 patients showed response, 7 patients showed progressive disease. Seven patients could not complete NACRT (6 patients lost to follow up and 1 expired due to cholangitis). Grade I, II and III chemotherapy related toxicity was seen in 10%, 1% and 2% of patients respectively. Group 2:‐24 patients (SURGERY ALONE).23/24 underwent surgery. In 21/24 patients, R0 margins were achieved,2 had metastatic disease and 1 is ready for surgery. Conclusion: In NACRT group, patients spent nearly 6 months from day of diagnosis to surgery as compared to upfront surgery group who spent around 34 days. 7/23 patients could not complete NACRT due to loss to follow up. Only 6/28 patients underwent resection in NACRT group while 23/24 were operated in the other group. Disease progression was seen in 7/13 while rest of the 6 patients showed tumor response with one complete pathological response. There was no postoperative pancreatic fistula, intraabdominal collection and chyle leak while POHS, blood loss and surgical site infection were less in patients receiving NACRT. Operative mortality was zero. (Figure Presented).
