DO  -  10.1245/s1043-022-11828-2
AB  -  INTRODUCTION: Previous comparisons of robotic versus distal pancreatectomy were limited by low number of cases. The aim of this study was to compare short and long term outcomes after laparoscopic (LDP) and robotic distal pancreatectomy (RDP). METHODS: Data from the 2010 to 2018 National Cancer Database (NCDB) was used to select patients treated with LDP or RDP for a pancreatic adenocarcinoma. Sociodemographics, and clinicopathologic factors (e.g. comorbidity, tumor stage/grade, lymph node resection, adjuvant therapy) were analyzed. RESULTS: Of the 1,636 patients, RDP was performed in 427 (26%) and LDP in 1,209 (74%). There were no sociodemographic differences between RDP and LDP, as well as preoperative cancer stage/grade. However, 23% received neoadjuvant therapy prior to RDP compared to 14.6% prior to LDP (p< 0.001). RDP achieved higher median lymph node yields RDP = 16 (IQR: 13) and LDP =14 (IQR: 11) and also resulted in lower conversion to open surgery, 12% vs 26% (p< 0.001). Similar rates of positive surgical margins in RDP and LDP (15% and 14% respectively, p=0.422), length of stay, 30 and 90 day mortalities were observed. In a multivariable survival analysis, older age, higher Charlson Deyo score, advanced tumor stage, and adjuvant chemoradiotherapy were associated with worse outcomes. Receipt of neoadjuvant chemoradiation, adjuvant chemotherapy, higher number of lymph node harvest, and robotic surgery were associated with better survival (Table 1). CONCLUSIONS: A 9 year analysis of NCDB data shows that RDP resulted in less conversion to open and statistically higher lymph node yields. Other short term outcomes in terms of margin status and 30 and 90 day mortalities appear similar. Surprisingly, after controlling for age, comorbidities, tumor stage/grade, neo & adjuvant therapy, RDP was associated with better survival. This may be due to more accurate staging with higher lymph node harvest. A randomized controlled trial is needed to further explain any differences between these approaches.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-02421615/full
