AB  -  Background: Excission Repair Cross‐Complementing 1 (ERCC‐1) has been shown to increase the resistance to platinum in some cancers. ERCC‐1 expression was investigated as a predictor of response to radiotherapy in patients (pt) with resected pancreatic cancer (RPC) undergoing pre or postoperative radiotherapy. Methods: A retrospective, non‐interventional study was conducted using data from the medical records of eligible RPC pt. Patients treated as part of clinical research trials were included. ERCC‐1 expression was determined by immunohistochemistry (IHQ) in primary or metastasis specimen. Results: Between January 1st 2007 and January 1st 2015, a total of 77 RPC pt were identified (median age 66 years old, 38 [49%] male, 73 [95%] ECOG 0‐1). 31 pt received perioperative chemo‐radiotherapy and 46 received adjuvant or neo‐adjuvant chemotherapy alone. Median follow‐up for patients was 31 months. ERCC‐1 expression did not predict overall Relapse‐Free Survival (RFS) or overall survival (OS) in the entire cohort or in the chemotherapy alone subgroup. In chemo‐radiotherapy subgroup, 13 ERCC‐1 negative pt presented longer RFS in comparison to 18 ERCC‐1 positive pt (ERCC‐1 negative 15,6 months [CI% 9,8‐21,2] vs. ERCC‐1 positive 3,8 months [CI% 2,45,3] log rank 18,419, p = 0,0001). Conclusions: Relapse‐Free Survival for RPC pt treated with radiotherapy was longer in ERCC‐1 negative patients compared with ERCC‐1 positive patients. Prospective randomized studies are mandatory to confirm ERCC‐1 expression clinical usefulness.
