AB  -  Background The impact of neoadjuvant chemotherapy (NAC) for locally marginally resectable pancreatic cancer is under debate. A phase II trial of neoadjuvant chemotherapy for borderline resectable (BR) and locally advanced unresectable (LA‐UR) pancreatic cancer is undergoing since Jan 2014 (UMIN000012480) using GSL (Gemcitabine 1000mg/m2, TS‐1 80mg/m2, LV 50mg), which have reported favorable results (33% of response rate, 93% of disease control rate and 16.6 months of median survival time) for unresectable pancreatic cancer (Cancer Chemother Pharmacol 2014; 74:911). Methods The subjects were pathologically proven pancreatic cancer diagnosed with BR and LA‐UR with short‐segment (=/<3cm) arterial abutment. At least two surgeons, two medical oncologists, and one radiologist joined a consensus meeting to decide the indication of NAC and evaluated the efficacy of NAC every month. Patients with CR, PR or SD after 2‐6 months NAC underwent curative surgery combined with dissection of the nerve plexus around major arteries. The primary endpoint was R0 resectional rate, and the secondary endpoints were resectional rate, response rate, adverse effect, surgical morbidity, mortality, operative time and hospital stay period. The proposed number of enrollment was 24 patients. Results Twenty‐four out of proposed 24 patients have undergone enrollment of NAC at the time of submission of the present abstract. Conclusion The primary endpoint of R0 resection rate, secondary endpoints and other short‐term outcomes after pancreatectomy will be presented in the coming meeting as well as the safety and efficacy of NAC‐GSL for BR and LA‐UR pancreatic cancer.
