AB  -  Introduction: This prospective double‐blind placebo controlled trial studied the benefits of intraoperative celiac neurolysis for durable postoperative pain control, long‐term quality of life and survival for patients with resected pancreatobiliary cancer. Method: Chemotherapy naïve patients undergoing resection for pancreatic adenocarcinoma or distal cholangiocarcinoma were randomized to receive either 50% ethanol (E) or saline (S) injection of the celiac plexus during pancreatoduodenectomy (PD). The primary study endpoint was postoperative pain severity over two years from randomization. Secondary endpoints included pain interference with daily life and overall survival. Results: Patient and disease characteristics between the two treatment groups are summarized in the table. Adverse events related to treatment did not differ between the two groups within 90 days of operation. Postoperative delayed gastric emptying was observed in 4 (14%) pts in the E group compared to 9% in the S group, p = 0.24. The mean pain severity at 12 mos for the E and S groups was 2.12 vs 2.42, p = 0.65, with no significant increases over respective baseline preoperative pain severity. Mean pain interference with daily life did not differ between the two treatments at any 3 month interval over two years. Actual survival for the entire study population at 2 yrs was 35%. Ethanol injection of the celiac plexus did not influence the timing or pattern of tumor recurrence, H.R.1.21, p = 0.57. Conclusions: Intraoperative chemical splanchnicectomy during PD for resectable pancreatobiliary cancer is safe but does not benefit patients with regards to long‐term pain control, pain interference with daily life, or disease‐free survival. [Figure presented]
