AB  -  Introduction: The ESPAC‐3 and CONKO‐001 trials established adjuvant GEM as the standard of care for resected pancreatic cancers based on the improved survival and acceptable toxicity. Aims: ESPAC‐4 aimed to determine whether combination chemotherapy with GEM/CAP improved survival compared to GEMmonotherapy. Patients & methods: Patients with pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma were randomized within 12 weeks of surgery (stratified for R0/R1 resection margin status and country) to have either 4 weeks of iv GEM alone or GEM with oral CAP for 6 months. The primary endpoint was overall survival; secondary endpoints were toxicity, relapse free survival, 2 and 5 year survival and quality of life. 722 patients (480 expected events), 361 in each arm, were needed to detect a 10% difference in 2 year survival rates with 90% power ( stratified log‐rank test with 5% two‐sided alpha). Results: Between Nov 10 2008 and Sep 11 2014, 732 patients were randomized (367 GEM, 365 GEM/CAP) with 435 deaths. Median age was 65 years, 57% were men. WHO performance status was 0, 1 or 2 in 42% 55% and 3% respectively. Preoperative median CA19‐9 was 143 KU/I. Median maximum tumor size was 30 mm, 60% were R1 resections, 80% were node positive and 24% were poorly differentiated. On Dec 11 2015 the Independent Trial Steering Committee requested that the trial proceed to full analysis. The data freeze will be made on March 1 2016. Conclusion: This is the largest adjuvant trial ever conducted comparing combination versus monotherapy for pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma. Full results will be reported at the meeting.
