AB  -  Rationale: We sought to investigate the influence of a Combined Exercise and Dietary Intervention (CEDI) during neoadjuvant chemotherapy on circulating cytokines and immune cells in patients with gastric/pancreatic cancer. Methods: A parallel randomized controlled open label trial (NCT05420259) was conducted at Hospital Beatriz Ângelo and Hospital da Luz. Twenty patients with gastric or pancreatic cancer eligible for neoadjuvant treatment were randomized to receive either 8 week CEDI, with moderate aerobic and resistance training with nutritional intervention or standard care. The levels of cytokines and immune cells were analysed in peripheral blood. Computed tomography derived body composition, nutritional and functional status and quality of life were assessed. Results: After neoadjuvant chemotherapy CEDI patients presented a lower median lower median CD56+/CD16+ (CEDI: 86.25,IQR:2.42 vs Control: 95.3,IQR: 2.65, p= 0.034), CD56bright/NKG2C+ (CEDI: 0, IQR: 0 vs Control:7.5, IQR:7.7, p=0.002) and CD56 dim/CD16+(CEDI:87.8 (14.25) vs 98.2(2.2),p=0.02). Baseline muscle radiation attenuation was postively correlated with CD56+/CD16+ (r=0.73, p<0.001). At follow up a significant positive correlation was found between subcutaneous adipose tissue (r=0.76,p=0.02) and CD56dim/NKG2C+. Only 2/6 operated patients in the CEDI group experienced postoperative complications grade I/II, whereas in the control group grade>III postoperative complications occurred in 5/9 patients (p=0.328). Further analysis revealed a near significant association between postoperative complications and IL‐10 (OR:1.60,95%CI: [1.08‐3.04], p=0.06), CD56bright/CD16+ (OR:1.93,95%CI:[1.11‐5.90], p=0.08) and CD56dim (OR:0.52,95%CI:[0.19‐0.87],p=0.06). Conclusion: CEDI and changes in body composition may modulate inflammatory response and influence final outcome. Disclosure of Interest: None declared
