AB  -  Background: Gemcitabine (Gem) monotherapy after surgery improves both survival rates and disease‐free survival (DFS) in patients with pancreatic cancer (PC). However, disease recurrence is common, suggesting a need for improved treatment. nab‐Paclitaxel (nab‐P) + Gem demonstrated superior efficacy over Gem alone in a phase III trial (MPACT) of patients with metastatic PC including the primary endpoint of overall survival (OS; median 8.7 vs 6.6 months; hazard ratio [HR] 0.72; P < 0.001). nab‐P + Gem also demonstrated longer independently assessed progression‐free survival (median 5.5 vs 3.7 months; HR 0.69; P < 0.001) and a higher overall response rate (23% vs 7%; response rate ratio 3.19; P < 0.001). Toxicities were manageable. Based on the MPACT trial findings, nab‐P + Gem will be compared with Gem alone in patients with resected PC. Trial design: Approximately 800 patients with histologically confirmed PC who undergo macroscopic complete resection (R0 or R1) with no evidence of metastasis will be randomized 1:1 to receive 6 cycles of nab‐P 125 mg/m2 + Gem 1000 mg/m2 or Gem alone 1000 mg/m2 on days 1, 8, and 15 of each 28‐day cycle. Other inclusion criteria are staging of T1‐3, N0‐1, M0; Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group performance status of 0 or 1; acceptable hematologic function; and carbohydrate antigen 19‐9 < 100 U/mL prior to randomization. Exclusion criteria include presence of neuroendocrine tumors or any other malignancy within 5 years of randomization, infection with HIV or hepatitis B or C, or prior neoadjuvant treatment or radiation therapy for PC. Randomization is stratified by resection status (R0 vs R1), nodal status (LN+ vs LN‐), and geographic region (North America, Europe, and Australia vs Asia Pacific). The primary endpoint is independently assessed DFS. Secondary endpoints are OS and safety. Exploratory endpoints include quality of life and molecular profiling of tumor tissue to correlate tumor heterogeneity with clinical outcome. Surgical tumor and normal specimens and blood samples will be collected from all patients at screening to help identify biomarkers predictive of treatment efficacy. Blood samples will also be collected at recurrence. At least 489 DFS events from 800 patients allow 90% power to detect a DFS HR of 0.74 at a 2‐sided significance level of 0.05. One interim safety analysis and 2 interim efficacy analyses (the first for futility and the second for both futility and efficacy) will be performed. Patient enrollment is ongoing (ClinicalTrials.gov: NCT01964430). Conclusions: The phase III APACT trial will determine whether nab‐P + Gem is superior to Gem alone as adjuvant treatment for patients with resected PC. Additionally, this study will establish one of the largest collections of primary tumor tissue from patients with PC for molecular analyses.
