DO  -  10.1016/j.ejso.2020.11.279
AB  -  Background: Approximately 15 ‐ 20% of gastric cancer patients present with unresectable (stage IVA) or metastatic (stage IVB) disease. They are associated with poor prognosis and are often treated with a non‐operative approach. There are few published literature on these patients being treated with a curative intent surgery after neoadjuvant treatment, which is being called as conversion therapy. However, there are conflicting results in other studies. Materials and Methods: We performed an analysis of data from our prospectively maintained surgical oncology database from May 2016 to May 2020. Histologically proven, stage IV, adenocarcinoma of the stomach, clinically unresectable (T4b) or with solitary metastasis (M1), who received neoadjuvant chemotherapy and underwent surgery with a pre‐operative curative intent, were considered as conversion surgery (CS) population. Stage IV patients who had not undergone curative resection were termed as non‐curative surgery/ population (NCS). The overall survival of the CS population was compared with Stage III curative resection patients in the database and the NCS population at the same center in the same period. Results: Of the 177 patients undergone surgical exploration for gastric cancer with the intent of curative surgery, 28 patients (15.8%) (M:F=2.5:1) were considered eligible as the CS population. The median age was 54 years (range: 23 to 70 years). Twenty‐three patients had T4b disease (14 patients infiltrating pancreas, 6 infiltrating both pancreas and liver, and 5 infiltrating diaphragm), and 5 patients had M1 disease (3 had localized peritoneal/omental deposits and 2 with hepatic metastasis). The most common neoadjuvant chemotherapy regimen used was EOX (32.1%), followed by DOX (28.5%) and FLOT (25%). Seventeen patients underwent an R0 resection with D2 lymphadenectomy (R0 resection rate: 60.7%). The median overall survival (OS) of the CS population undergoing R0 resection was 23.3 months, which was similar to the median OS of 24.7 months in stage III patients (n=40) undergoing R0 resection (p=0.59; HR:1.26 with 95% CI: 0.53 to 2.95). The median OS of the NCS population was 15.1 months. There was a trend towards lower survival in the NCS population compared to the CS population (p=0.05; HR:2.51 with 95% CI: 0.98 to 6.41). Conclusions: A subset of stage IV gastric cancer patients with solitary metastasis, or limited surrounding organ invasion, may have improved survival if surgical resection with R0 resection is achieved after neoadjuvant chemotherapy. The survival of this population may achieve outcomes similar to stage III gastric cancer. However, further randomized controlled trials are required to substantiate the results.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-02246407/full
