DO  -  10.1159/000498929
AB  -  Background: In most cases an immediate radical re‐resection (IRR) after simple cholecystectomy in incidental gallbladder carcinoma (IGBC) is needed. The biggest biliary platform in Europe‐the German‐Registry (GR), shows positive results for T1b with IRR since 2007. Results for T2‐3 are disappointing even after IRR. Questions? Is there a potential benefit due to a multimodal platform for these kind of rare cancers. Materials and Methods: For data analysis we used the GR. First author is the PI of the GR. The GR includes more than 1100 cases of IGBC. Secondly the talk will present new multicenter trial in trial concept, including the phase III GAIN‐trial currently starting in Germany in 50 centers with a DFG grant. GAIN is a randomized multicenter phase III study for T2‐3 IGBC's an resectable and borderline resectable biliary tract cancers (BTC), evaluating the role of systemic therapy in a multimodal setting before and after IRR in IGBC or liver and bile duct resection in BTC. After cholecystectomy and diagnosis of IGBC patients will be randomized into arm A: 3 cycles gemcitabine/cisplatin chemotherapy followed by radical liver resection + lymphnode dissection (IGBC) or radical lver and bile duct resection in BTC followed by 3 cycles gem/cis. If patients are randomized in arm B they will receive the standard of care, radical re‐resection without systemic therapy. If pts are oncologically to advanced and candidates for 1st line the will be directly randomized in another 1st line trila (trial‐in‐trial concept). All IGBC's in addition will be directly registered in the GR. Results: In the GR (n > 1100 pts) in T1b‐T3 cases there was a significant survival benefit for patients with IRR. Liver Wedge resection (WRT) showed good data in T1b and T2. For T3 more radical techniques showed better results. Less than 50% of T2‐3 tumors in the registry have been re‐resected. LR was performed significantly more often in High‐volume (HV) clinics. Conclusion: There is a significant benefit after radical re‐resection in T1b‐T3 IGBC's but the results in T2‐3 cancers are still disappointing like in the whole entity of BTC's. So a more radical approach is needed. Therefore the multimodal multicenter phase 3 trial GAIN currently starts in 30 centers in Germany. Another 20 centers of the GR network will start in the near future. The trial is supported by the Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft‐DFG (German Research Foundation) and is also supported by the German association of medical oncologists (AIO) and the German association of liver biliary and pancreatic surgeons‐CALGP. Due to the trial in trial concepts patients screened for GAIN but are candidates for 1st line will be directly included in another 1st line trial.
