AB  -  PURPOSE/HYPOTHESIS: Now the third leading cause of US cancer deaths, pancreatic cancer (PanC) incidence is rising. Surgery is the only chance at cure but with a high risk of perioperative complications. Sarcopenia, highly prevalent in PanC, predicts poor outcomes and can limit adjuvant chemotherapy options. Preoperative exercise (“prehab”) benefits other cancers, but the effect in the brief preoperative PanC period is unknown, as is adherence to home‐based approach in this population. PURPOSE: To report adherence results from the feasibility phase of a pilot randomized controlled trial capitalizing on a 2‐ to 3‐ week preoperative window between diagnosis of PanC and resection by pancreaticoduodenectomy (PD). In this innovative, short‐term approach, survivors are encouraged to become as fit as possible for surgery through a condensed period of high‐volume exercise. Given anticipated symptom burden combined with the gravity of PanC diagnosis, we anticipate adherence below that published for other cancers. NUMBER OF SUBJECTS: A total of 30 patients (46.7% female; 100% white; age range, 39‐81 years; mean = 66.5 ± 10.6 years), approved for surgical resection of PanC or related premalignancy by PD and not already exercising at a higher level, enrolled in the feasibility phase were randomized to 1 of 2 prehab approaches differing only by strengthening (SPRE, two‐thirds of the sample) or active range of motion (AROM) (NPRE, one‐third of the sample). MATERIALS/METHODS: All participants received a single, inperson physical therapy (PT) session for a home exercise program of endurance training (seated ergometer), plus 7 to 8 exercises of either AROM or strengthening (adjustable arm and leg weights), all with a daily goal of 60 minutes by surgery, at RPE 13. Each prescription was personalized while following a general protocol of exercise options to target large muscle groups. Survivors received 2 to 3 follow‐up phone calls over the intervention, with adherence interview at each, and kept a diary. Adherence (%) was calculated as: number of days exercise was performed/total days in the intervention period. Consistent with published cancer exercise adherence articles, 75% to 100% adherence was categorized as high (full), 50% to 74% as moderate (mod), and less than 50% as minimal (min). Small group sizes prevent calculation of 95% confidence interval. RESULTS: Adherence as full, mod, and min with endurance training was 68.4%, 21.1%, and 10.5% for the SPRE group and 88.9%, 0%, and 11.1% for the NPRE group. AROM adherence was 88.9% (full), 11.1%, and 0% and for strength was 78.9%, 5.3%, and 15.8%. CONCLUSIONS: Even with a PanC diagnosis and active symptoms of fatigue and pain, our sample achieved greater adherence than that published in other cancers with low survival. Family buy‐in, direct surgeon recommendation, and phone follow‐up were facilitators. Caregiving roles were barriers. Having achieved higher adherence than with similar prescription in our study of elderly ovarian cancer survivors on chemotherapy, we suspect the high stakes, brief time frame, and use of a treatment‐free period are key. CLINICAL RELEVANCE: High adherence may be achieved with high‐volume, home‐based endurance and strength training in the 2 to 3 weeks before PD. At least 1 follow‐up phone call is recommended.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-01620087/full
