DO  -  10.1016/j.clnesp.2020.09.481
AB  -  Rationale: Intervention programs in cancer patients have been proposed with the aim of increasing tolerance and decreasing toxicity of treatments. The purpose of this study was to study the adherence of gastrointestinal cancer patients to a Combined Exercise and Dietary Intervention (CEDI) during neoadjuvant chemotherapy. Methods: Open label randomized controlled trial, conducted from April 2018 to November 2019. The intervention group received individualized combined exercise intervention, with dietary counseling and one oral nutritional supplement per day (CEDI). Demographic data and clinical data were retrieved from electronic records. Nutritional Assessment was conducted with anthropometric and bioelectrical impedance measures, and the Patient Generated Subjective Global Assessment. Performance status was assessed using Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group scale and handgrip strength was measured. Quality of life was evaluated with the European Organization for the Research and Treatment of Cancer (EORTC) QLQ‐ C30 questionnaire. Serum C‐Reactive Protein (CRP), albumin and total proteins were recorded. All assessments were conducted at baseline and after 8 weeks. In order to address patients adherence to CEDI, the number of patients that accepted to participate and that adhered to the oral nutritional supplements and exercise was recorded. Results: Patients (n=40) were randomly allocated to intervention or controlled arm (20 patients per group), two patients refused CEDI. 28 (73.7%) were male, with median age of 63.4 (IQR:12); 8 (21.1%) and 30 (78,9%) had disease stage II and III, respectively. Disease location was as follows: 3 (7.9%) esophagus, 17 (44.7 %) gastric, 5 (13.2%) pancreas and 13 ( 34.2%) rectum. All patients underwent neoadjuvant treatment, that varied according to disease location, and consisted of chemotherapy or chemo and radiotherapy. A total of 29 patients have completed the study follow up. Patients were well matched in regard to all analyzed variables except for ECOG scale (intervention‐ ECOG 0: 8 (38.9%), ECOG 1: 8 (44.4%) ECOG 2: 2 (16.7%) vs. control: ‐ ECOG 0: 12 (57.9%), ECOG 1: 2 (10.5%) ECOG 2: 6(31.6%). At first 18/20 (89.5%) of patients accepted to participate in CEDI, but 1 patient dropped out on the second week of follow up. In general 12/17 (70.6%) adhered to nutrition supplementation (one supplement per day was prescribed, and supplement intake ≥ 4 weeks was considered acceptable) and 11/17 (64.7%) to exercise program. Although no statistically significant association was found between disease site, gastric cancer patients had a higher adherence to oral nutritional supplements and exercise. Patients in the intervention arm were able to maintain weight and waist circumference during neoadjuvant therapy, in contrast to patients in the control arm who lost a mean weight of 3.34kg (3.34±5.89 vs. 0.59±2.30, p=0.03) and waist circumference of 4.2 cm (1.66±3.98 vs. ‐4.2±6.61, p=0.012). Although no differences between study groups was found for bioelectrical impedance measurements, phase angle reduced significantly in the control arm (baseline: 6.11±0.76 vs. 8 weeks: 5.29 ±1.29, p=0.004). Functional domain from the quality of life questionnaire only improved significantly for the intervention arm (baseline: 74.73± 18.63 vs. 8 weeks: 84.97±10.23, p=0.017). Conclusion: This study has demonstrated that a Combined Exercise and Dietary Intervention (CEDI) is feasible in patients with gastrointestinal cancer under neoadjuvant therapy. Further efforts should be conducted in order to maximize patient’s adherence. Disclosure of Interest: None declared
