DO  -  10.1016/j.ejso.2020.11.162
AB  -  Background: At presentation, 50% of patients with gastric cancer have metastases, 40% of which are peritoneal metastases. Cytoreductive surgery (CRS) and heated intraperitoneal chemotherapy (HIPEC) was first described as a treatment for metastatic gastric cancer patients in 19891. In 2019, a randomised multicentre trial on CRS and HIPEC in these patients demonstrated a significant benefit in the treatment arm with a median overall survival of 18.6 months and 5‐year survival of 20.2%2. Materials and Methods: A gastric cancer program was started at the peritoneal malignancy unit at the Mater Misericordiae Hospital in July 2019. Advice from other international centres was sought in devising a protocol for patients. Patients were assessed based on their age, fitness, treatment history, the extent of disease on initial presentation, and response to treatment. Results: In the first year, a total of seven patients underwent CRS and HIPEC for metastatic gastric cancer. The average age was 61 years. All patients received neoadjuvant chemotherapy. Three patients had metastasis proven in cytology of peritoneal washings, 3 had biopsy proven peritoneal deposits and 1 had both positive peritoneal deposits and peritoneal washings. At surgery, 5 of 7 patients required extended resections which included either spleen, pancreas, or colon. One patient had a grade 3 complication (anastomotic leak) requiring insertion of an IR guided drain. The average length of operation was 412 minutes with an average length of stay of 22 days. All patients had poorly differentiated lymph node‐positive adenocarcinoma with a poor tumour regression grade. Five patients received adjuvant treatment. To date, 4 patients have disease recurrence 2 of whom are deceased. The average time to recurrence was approximately 7 months. Despite a diagnosis of Stage 4 gastric cancer, three of 7 patients remain disease‐free. Conclusions: Cytoreductive surgery and HIPEC is safe in selected patients with peritoneal metastases from gastric cancer. References 1. Cunliffe WJ, Sugarbaker PH: Gastrointestinal malignancy:rationale for adjuvant therapy using early postoperative intraperitoneal chemotherapy. Br J Surg 1989;76:1082–1090. 2. Bonnot PE, Piessen G, Kepenekian V et al.Cytoreductive Surgery With or Without Hyperthermic Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy for Gastric Cancer With Peritoneal Metastases (CYTO‐CHIP study): A Propensity Score Analysis. J Clin Oncol. 2019 Aug 10;37(23):2028‐2040. Head & neck and thyroid cancer
