DO  -  10.1016/S0302-2838(21)01192-1
AB  -  Introduction & Objectives: Few data from prospective randomized trials are available reporting oncological outcomes of robotic radical cystectomy (RC) with intracorporeal urinary diversion versus the open approach. We present 2‐yr oncological outcomes from a prospective randomized study comparing open (ORC) versus robotic (RRC) approach, assessing between group differences in terms of site of metastasis. Materials & Methods: Between Jan 2018 and Sept 2020, 116 patients with cT2‐4N0M0, or recurrent high‐grade non‐muscle invasive bladder cancer (BC) underwent a covariate adaptive randomization process based on BMI, ASA score, preoperative hgb, urinary diversion, neoadiuvant chemotherapy and cT‐stage (clinicaltrials.gov/show/NCT03434132) and received ORC or RRC. Student‐t and Chi‐Square tests were used for continuous and categorical variables, respectively. Two‐yr disease‐free (DFS), metastasis‐free (MFS) and cancer‐specific survival (CSS) were compared between cohorts using log rank test. Univariable and multivariable regression analyses were applied to identify independent predictors of survival. Sites of recurrence were reported and compared between ORC and RRC groups. Results: Clinical and pathological features were reported. Two‐yr DFS, MFS and CSS were 70.9%, 76.3% and 96.4% versus 86%, 85.9% and 78.8% for ORC and RRC, respectively (log rank p=0.52, p=0.49 and p=0.15, respectively. At multivariable analysis pTstage>2 (HR 6.09 95%CI 2.1‐17.1, p=0.001) and lymph‐node density (HR 1.02; 1.001‐1.037, p=0.035) were independent predictors of DFS; pathologic extravesical disease was the only independent predictor of MFS (pT>2 HR 8.63 95% CI 2.65‐28.69, p=0.001). At a median f‐up of 14 months a total of 27 metastases occurred in 17 patients, 17 and 10 in ORC and RRC cohort, respectively. Sites of metastasis were lung, liver, brain, nodes, pancreas, skin/ports and peritoneum (Fig.) with a slight trend towards different distribution between ORC and RRC (p=0.085). Incidence of pulmonary (40%) and bone metastasis (26.7%) was higher in ORC cohort, while incidence of visceral (30%) and peritoneal (30%) metastasis was higher in RRC cohort (Fig.). (Figure Presented) Conclusions: ORC and RRC showed comparable oncological outcomes. Site‐specific metastatic spread following robotic or open approach should be confirmed with longer follow‐up and would require specific investigation to understand molecular basis of events.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-02372405/full
